One important clarification. The asterisk(*) is an attribute of the variable not the type which your explanation seems to suggest. This distinction is important because if you consider the following line:
int* x, y;
x is a pointer to an int while y is just an int. If you wanted both to be pointers you would need to write something like:
int *x, *y;

I want to add, for people trying to understand why the * usage is different when by a type. It's not. The asterisk just isn't by the type, its by the name. 

int * x;    
is not saying x is an int pointer, its saying that dereferenced x is an int. 

This helped me immensely when I realized it, and it explains:
int * x, y;    
makes x a pointer and y an int since y isnt dereferenced but x is in the declaration.

Very good video.

Th key points here are passing by value vs passing by reference. Passing a regular variable foo(x) copies the value of x, whereas foo(&x) passes x by reference by giving a memory address. & is simply used to get the address of a variable which essentially gives you a pointer. A pointer is just a memory address.  If you need a reference you can pass a memory address of a variable using & or you can pass a pointer. A pointers value is a memory address. Using & on a pointer returns the memory address that the pointer (memory address) is stored at. So:

Int *x = new int(5);
Int address_of_address = &x;

Address of address contains a memory address, whose value contains a pointer (which is a memory address), which contains the value 5. So address of address contains the memory location of that pointer on the stack. 

The visual of the table with the address on one side and the value on the other side is how you have to picture it. Just ask yourself are you accessing the right side or the left side?

2:30

std::unique_ptr<Human> ptrJeff(new Human);
When using the new keyword, allocate is still done to the heap like a normal pointer. Consider this instead:
std::unique_ptr<Human> ptrJeff = std::make_unique<Human>();
Or, even:
auto ptrJeff = std::make_unique<Human>();

This is especially important for shared pointers. For unique pointers, the only difference in the end is exception handling.

Also, smart pointers need #include <memory>.
Another thing to keep in mind is that these were added in c++11, so it might even be a good idea to specify. -std=c++11 or later.

